{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01S2ECnKJVVSgodjRYiBvZVH","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t//! Translation of `quic/tls_api.h`.\n2\t//!\n3\t//! `tls_api.h` is the boundary between quic-core's QUIC machinery\n4\t//! and the TLS 1.3 stack underneath.  It exposes the master\n5\t//! TLS context lifecycle, per-connection TLS contexts, the\n6\t//! retry-token / retry-protection helpers, and a handful of\n7\t//! provider-installation entry points kept in this header so\n8\t//! applications don't have to include `tls.h`.\n9\t//!\n10\t//! Phase 4: core bodies are implemented.  Backend-specific TLS\n11\t//! handshakes use the trait objects in [`crate::tls`], while the QUIC\n12\t//! Initial, retry-token, retry-integrity, reset-secret, and test\n13\t//! crypto helpers are implemented here with RustCrypto primitives.\n14\t//!\n15\t//! ## Shape conventions\n16\t//!\n17\t//! * Free C functions whose first argument is a `Quic*` or `Connection*`\n18\t//!   become inherent methods on [`Quic`] / [`Connection`].\n19\t//! * The C `void*` AEAD / PN-encryption / cipher / hash contexts\n20\t//!   are gone — Phase 2 replaced them with the trait family in\n21\t//!   [`crate::tls`] (`PacketKey`, `HeaderKey`, `Session`,\n22\t//!   `ClientConfig`, `ServerConfig`).  Backend implementations\n23\t//!   (picotls, rustls, OpenSSL) wrap their native session types\n24\t//!   to satisfy those traits.  No `unsafe fn` or raw-pointer\n25\t//!   parameters remain in this module.\n26\t//! * `int` flag parameters that encode booleans (`is_enc`,\n27\t//!   `is_client`, `client_mode`, `use_low_memory`, `reset`,\n28\t//!   `check_reuse`, `sending`) become `bool`.\n29\t//! * C status-code returns (`int` 0/-1 / `ERROR_*`) become\n30\t//!   `Result<T, Error>`.  The `SIZE_MAX` failure sentinel returned\n31\t//!   by `aead_decrypt_*` becomes `Err(Error::AeadCheck)` instead of\n32\t//!   leaking the sentinel through the success arm.\n33\t//! * Owning `PtlsIovec* get_certs_from_file(…, size_t* count)`\n34\t//!   collapses to `Option<Vec<Vec<u8>>>`: the C callee `malloc`s\n35\t//!   the slot array and writes its length through the out-pointer,\n36\t//!   and the caller `free`s.  A Rust `Vec` carries both ownership\n37\t//!   and length, with `Drop` taking the place of the manual free.\n38\t//! * Output parameters (`uint8_t reset_secret[16]`,\n39\t//!   `uint8_t* master_secret`, `void** aead_ctx`, `size_t*\n40\t//!   text_length`, `int* data_consumed`, `int* is_new_token`,\n41\t//!   `ConnectionId* odcid`, `size_t* token_size`, `size_t* length`)\n42\t//!   become `&mut`-borrowed slots or are returned as part of the\n43\t//!   function's result tuple, depending on whether the caller\n44\t//!   already holds storage.\n45\t//! * `struct sockaddr*` arguments map to [`core::net::SocketAddr`]\n46\t//!   for parity with `quic.rs`.\n47\t//! * `FreeVerifyCertificateCtx` is a function-pointer typedef in\n48\t//!   `quic.h`; the trait counterpart is already in `quic.rs` and is\n49\t//!   reused here.\n50\t//! * Function-pointer parameters in callbacks\n51\t//!   ([`Quic::tls_set_verify_certificate_callback`]) take\n52\t//!   `Option<Box<dyn …>>` — the C `NULL` sentinel maps to `None`\n53\t//!   and the registered callback is owned by the registry.\n54\t//!\n55\t//! ## Initial-secret label constants\n56\t//!\n57\t//! `LABEL_QUIC_BASE` is `#define`d as `NULL` in C — it's the \"no\n58\t//! prefix label\" sentinel passed to tls.  In Rust the prefix-label\n59\t//! parameters that accept it become `Option<&str>`, so the constant\n60\t//! itself doesn't appear; the call sites pass `None` when they would\n61\t//! have passed `LABEL_QUIC_BASE`.\n62\t//!\n63\t//! ## Public vs. internal `set_*` methods\n64\t//!\n65\t//! `picoquic.h` exposes `picoquic_set_verify_certificate_callback`,\n66\t//! `picoquic_set_client_authentication`, and\n67\t//! `picoquic_set_use_exporter` as thin wrappers around the\n68\t//! `picoquic_tls_set_*` functions declared here.  The wrappers live\n69\t//! on [`Quic`] without the `tls_` prefix (see `lib.rs`); the\n70\t//! TLS-side methods in this module keep the `tls_` prefix so both\n71\t//! pairs can coexist.\n72\t//!\n73\t//! ## Deliberate omissions\n74\t//!\n75\t//! * The five `#if 0`-disabled `cid_*_under_mask_ctx` /\n76\t//!   `cid_free_encrypt_global_ctx` entries are dead code in the C\n77\t//!   source and are not translated.\n78\t//! * `get_private_key_from_file` is also `#if 0` in the header (and\n79\t//!   the `_t` callback variant lives in `crypto_provider_api.rs`);\n80\t//!   not translated."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0e5c1330-a988-493e-903c-214774c62736","uuid":"05b94e70-0b47-4357-ac63-9477156a51a4","timestamp":"2026-05-07T06:06:41.792Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/richbarn/Projects/picoquic/rs/fq/src/tls_api.rs","content":"//! Translation of `quic/tls_api.h`.\n//!\n//! `tls_api.h` is the boundary between quic-core's QUIC machinery\n//! and the TLS 1.3 stack underneath.  It exposes the master\n//! TLS context lifecycle, per-connection TLS contexts, the\n//! retry-token / retry-protection helpers, and a handful of\n//! provider-installation entry points kept in this header so\n//! applications don't have to include `tls.h`.\n//!\n//! Phase 4: core bodies are implemented.  Backend-specific TLS\n//! handshakes use the trait objects in [`crate::tls`], while the QUIC\n//! Initial, retry-token, retry-integrity, reset-secret, and test\n//! crypto helpers are implemented here with RustCrypto primitives.\n//!\n//! ## Shape conventions\n//!\n//! * Free C functions whose first argument is a `Quic*` or `Connection*`\n//!   become inherent methods on [`Quic`] / [`Connection`].\n//! * The C `void*` AEAD / PN-encryption / cipher / hash contexts\n//!   are gone — Phase 2 replaced them with the trait family in\n//!   [`crate::tls`] (`PacketKey`, `HeaderKey`, `Session`,\n//!   `ClientConfig`, `ServerConfig`).  Backend implementations\n//!   (picotls, rustls, OpenSSL) wrap their native session types\n//!   to satisfy those traits.  No `unsafe fn` or raw-pointer\n//!   parameters remain in this module.\n//! * `int` flag parameters that encode booleans (`is_enc`,\n//!   `is_client`, `client_mode`, `use_low_memory`, `reset`,\n//!   `check_reuse`, `sending`) become `bool`.\n//! * C status-code returns (`int` 0/-1 / `ERROR_*`) become\n//!   `Result<T, Error>`.  The `SIZE_MAX` failure sentinel returned\n//!   by `aead_decrypt_*` becomes `Err(Error::AeadCheck)` instead of\n//!   leaking the sentinel through the success arm.\n//! * Owning `PtlsIovec* get_certs_from_file(…, size_t* count)`\n//!   collapses to `Option<Vec<Vec<u8>>>`: the C callee `malloc`s\n//!   the slot array and writes its length through the out-pointer,\n//!   and the caller `free`s.  A Rust `Vec` carries both ownership\n//!   and length, with `Drop` taking the place of the manual free.\n//! * Output parameters (`uint8_t reset_secret[16]`,\n//!   `uint8_t* master_secret`, `void** aead_ctx`, `size_t*\n//!   text_length`, `int* data_consumed`, `int* is_new_token`,\n//!   `ConnectionId* odcid`, `size_t* token_size`, `size_t* length`)\n//!   become `&mut`-borrowed slots or are returned as part of the\n//!   function's result tuple, depending on whether the caller\n//!   already holds storage.\n//! * `struct sockaddr*` arguments map to [`core::net::SocketAddr`]\n//!   for parity with `quic.rs`.\n//! * `FreeVerifyCertificateCtx` is a function-pointer typedef in\n//!   `quic.h`; the trait counterpart is already in `quic.rs` and is\n//!   reused here.\n//! * Function-pointer parameters in callbacks\n//!   ([`Quic::tls_set_verify_certificate_callback`]) take\n//!   `Option<Box<dyn …>>` — the C `NULL` sentinel maps to `None`\n//!   and the registered callback is owned by the registry.\n//!\n//! ## Initial-secret label constants\n//!\n//! `LABEL_QUIC_BASE` is `#define`d as `NULL` in C — it's the \"no\n//! prefix label\" sentinel passed to tls.  In Rust the prefix-label\n//! parameters that accept it become `Option<&str>`, so the constant\n//! itself doesn't appear; the call sites pass `None` when they would\n//! have passed `LABEL_QUIC_BASE`.\n//!\n//! ## Public vs. internal `set_*` methods\n//!\n//! `picoquic.h` exposes `picoquic_set_verify_certificate_callback`,\n//! `picoquic_set_client_authentication`, and\n//! `picoquic_set_use_exporter` as thin wrappers around the\n//! `picoquic_tls_set_*` functions declared here.  The wrappers live\n//! on [`Quic`] without the `tls_` prefix (see `lib.rs`); the\n//! TLS-side methods in this module keep the `tls_` prefix so both\n//! pairs can coexist.\n//!\n//! ## Deliberate omissions\n//!\n//! * The five `#if 0`-disabled `cid_*_under_mask_ctx` /\n//!   `cid_free_encrypt_global_ctx` entries are dead code in the C\n//!   source and are not translated.\n//! * `get_private_key_from_file` is also `#if 0` in the header (and\n//!   the `_t` callback variant lives in `crypto_provider_api.rs`);\n//!   not translated.","numLines":80,"startLine":1,"totalLines":1741}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_01T5NisM7voCroz9pDXotTFS","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have a good understanding of the situation. Let me think through each of the 5 items:\n\n1. **`picoquic_is_ech_handshake`** (lib.rs:3479) - The C code delegates to `ptls_is_ech_handshake(tls_ctx->tls, NULL, NULL, NULL)`. The Rust Session trait doesn't have this method. I need to:\n   - Add `fn is_ech_handshake(&self) -> bool` to the `Session` trait\n   - Implement it on `Connection` by checking if the TLS context exists and calling the method on it\n   - Fix the comment referencing the C code\n\n2. **`picoquic_ech_get_retry_config`** (lib.rs:3487) - The C code accesses the retry configs buffer and length from the TLS context. I need to add a method to the `Session` trait that exposes the retry configs as a byte slice, then implement it on `Connection` to return the appropriate data.\n\n3. **`picoquic_ech_get_kem_from_curve`** (ech.rs) - The C code searches through an array of HPKE KEMs to find one matching a given group ID, then returns the KEM identifier. I need to create a function that takes a group ID and returns the corresponding KEM ID by iterating through the available KEMs.\n\nMapping the group IDs to their corresponding KEM IDs...\n\nNow I'm looking at the `picoquic_parse_public_key_asn1` function, which handles ASN.1 parsing to extract the algorithm OID, parameters, and the public key bit string using picotls helper functions.\n\nFor the simpler `picoquic_fastcc_delay_threshold` function, I need to create a new `fastcc.rs` module and add it to lib.rs since it's not currently there. Let me check the existing module declarations to see where it should fit.\n\nFor the ECH-related functions, both `is_ech_handshake` and `ech_get_retry_config` are currently stubbed out with TLS wiring markers. They need to access the `tls_ctx` field, which is modeled as an optional boxed `Session` trait object in Rust, so I'll need to add methods that can interact with that.\n\nLet me examine the Session trait to understand what methods are already available and what I need to add.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3108,"cache_read_input_tokens":44189,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3108},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0e5c1330-a988-493e-903c-214774c62736","uuid":"13fdedf7-20e8-4367-9f35-874133db36ca"}
