g an algorithm identifier SEQUENCE with an OID and parameters, followed by a BIT STRING for the actual public key data. The tricky part is that the C function returns consumed bytes while also providing sliced references into the buffer, so I'll need to track offsets carefully as I parse through each TLV element.\n\nWriting ASN.1 parser... Writing the ASN.1 parser... Finishing the ASN.1 parsing logic... Parsing the ASN.1 structure further...\n\nI'm extracting the BIT STRING containing the public key data, then validating that we've consumed exactly all the bytes in the outer sequence to catch any structural errors. The Rust version needs to replicate this same validation logic where the public key algorithm is stored as the OID value.\n\nWriting the ASN.1 parser... Writing the parser struct... Parsing the ASN.1 structure for the public key certificate...\n\nNow I'm mapping this back to the C implementation to verify the correspondence—the outer sequence bounds, the OID algorithm identifier, the parameter bytes, and the BIT STRING all align with how the C code extracts and returns these values. But I'm realizing the C function doesn't return an error code directly; instead it returns the byte index and uses an output parameter for the error state.\n\nLooking at how the caller actually uses this function, it ignores the return value and only checks the error parameter, so wrapping everything into a Rust `Result` type is the more idiomatic approach here.\n\nNow I'm starting a new file for the FastCC congestion control algorithm...\n\nDefining the delay threshold function...\n\nI should add the module to lib.rs and double-check the comment style to match what's already in the codebase. Let me look back at the C implementation for `ech_get_retry_config` to understand what needs translating next.\n\nThe retry config lives in the TLS context's retry configs field, which means the Rust version should delegate to the `Session` trait rather than storing it directly in the con