ith no parameters. I'm verifying the secp256r1 structure by tracing through the DER encoding—the outer SEQUENCE contains an inner SEQUENCE with the algorithm OID, and I'm checking that my position calculations align correctly for extracting the parameters. I'm verifying that the C code correctly extracts the parameter bytes by setting the base pointer to the position right after the OID value and calculating the length as the remaining bytes in the inner sequence, which for secp256r1 gives us those 10 bytes starting with the OID tag. Looking at the secp384r1 structure, I can see the pattern continues with the algorithm OID being 7 bytes and the curve OID following after, confirming my implementation correctly handles the full TLV encoding for both curve types. I'm verifying my implementation handles X25519 correctly—the inner sequence is 5 bytes, the OID takes up positions 4-9, leaving an empty parameter slice as expected. Now I need to check the comparison logic in `picoquic_ech_parse_public_key` to make sure it's matching the 3-byte X25519 OID correctly.\n\nThe OID comparison extracts just the value bytes without the tag or length, which aligns with how I'm slicing the input. My implementation looks solid. Time to start writing the actual code changes across the five files: adding the Session trait methods, fixing the ECH functions, implementing the ASN.1 parsing helpers, creating the fastcc module, and wiring it all together.\n\nI notice `Error` is imported in ech.rs but won't be needed in fastcc.rs. Looking at the existing `kem_id` constants already defined in ech.rs, I can see the KEM IDs are already there...\n\nFor the TLS group IDs, I could add a separate `group_id` module with the named constants, but I'll keep it simpler and just use the literal values with comments instead of over-engineering it. Now I'm ready to write the actual implementation.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_c